 - NEGATIVE EVIDENCE: `M1RuntimeHarnessTests.productionConfigurationGate` names the production `HarnessApplication.run` call site, proves the exact repetition bound by admitting 8 and refusing 9, and refuses hidden controls, an exposed profile, general UDP, repeated fault injection, and missing revision evidence. Failure tests traverse the real command entry point and require exact nonzero codes rather than calling component guards directly.
 - MUTATION ANOMALY/FIX: The first `8 -> 7` cap narrowing remained green because the test derived both accepted and rejected values from the production constant, making its evidence self-minted. The test now owns independent contract literals 8 and 9. With production still narrowed to 7 the complete M1 harness suite exited 1; after restoring 8 it passed. Two earlier probes that returned 0 are retained and explicitly do not count as negative evidence.
 - RESOURCE/PRIVACY EVIDENCE: Each generation uses real process-owned socket-pair descriptors and a controlled native borrow, joins the packet read and bridge tasks, closes the TCP channel, and requires zero descriptor, task, channel, socket, and native-runtime residue. The fixture manifest and validator reject dependency drift, raw profile output, nonzero growth gauges, unexpected stdout/stderr, and exit drift without retaining packet, DNS, endpoint, credential, or profile data.
+- REVIEW REWORK: Revision 1 exposed two self-minted claims. Mid-session loss called `stop(.providerFailure)` before checking `.failed`, so a dropped SSH/DNS health event still passed; the host-owner sentinel was an unrelated empty object. `M1RuntimeHarnessCommand.run` now waits for the coordinator-produced failure before the idempotent cleanup stop. The simulated host owner now owns the real `HarnessCoreComposition` bootstrap boundary, is released after runtime readiness, and must deallocate before runtime-owned TCP/DNS forwarding begins.
+- REWORK NEGATIVE EVIDENCE: Narrowing SSH and DNS `healthSink.receive` delivery to impossible generation zero made their named `HarnessApplication.run` production-entry tests exit `1` after the bounded one-second observation deadline. Narrowing host-owner release to impossible generation zero made the named forwarding/ownership test exit `1`. The source was restored from a byte-identical task-scoped copy after every mutant and returned to SHA-256 `f247f10825bcb255ad08adbc9735d29067751ec6c5d7b482128c40c53097c5c5`.
+- REWORK VERIFICATION: `make m1-runtime-harness-test` passes six focused tests and all seven manifest rows. Two additional unchanged-binary manifest runs also pass 7/7 with every row privacy-safe and exact exits `0/70/71/72/73/74/74`. The complete harness filter passes 38 tests in three suites; the full SwiftPM run passes 514 tests in 44 suites with the existing 25 known unavailable-ReluxNIOSSH issues. Strict task-scoped Swift format, Core boundaries, fixture build, and diff checks exit `0`. One invalid Go-style `swift test ... -count=1` invocation exited `64` before tests and was immediately replaced by the valid Swift invocation; it is retained as command-error evidence rather than reported as a test failure.
